    <Primary_Result_Summary>
      <purpose>Science</purpose>
      <processing_level>Derived</processing_level>
      <description>
        Due to the tilt of the Moon's spin axis (1.54 degrees), some regions
        near the poles are in permanent shadow while other nearby regions remain
        illuminated for the majority of the year. This subtle tilt causes
        lighting changes from lunation to lunation. The poles are most
        illuminated at their respective summer solstice. Thus, images for the
        NAC south pole mosaic were acquired centered on the southern solstice
        (2010-07-23 through 2010-12-11), and layered in order of the greatest
        incidence angle and sub-solar latitude closest to the pole. The mosaic
        has a latitude range of -90 to -85.5 degrees and is stored as 24 polar
        stereographic map tiles. The tiles are in latitudinal bands of 1.5, 1,
        1, and 1 degrees radiating from the pole. There are four tiles in the
        first band (-88.5 to -90 degrees latitude), eight in the second (-87.5
        to -88.5 degrees latitude), twelve in the third (-86.5 to -87.5 degrees
        latitude), and sixteen in the fourth (-85.5 to -86.5 degrees latitude).
        NAC images were map projected onto the GLD100 DEM using the LOLA
        crossover corrected ephemeris. The images were not registered to one
        another. Stereographic projection at 1 meter/px centered at 90S, 0E with
        a radius of 1737400 m. The center of this tile is at 86.0S, 33.7E. For
        more information see [WAGNERETAL2015].
      </description>
    </Primary_Result_Summary>
